<h2>The case and the custody</h2>
<p>The FIR, registered by forest authorities in Bhavnagar, alleged conscious possession of ambergris recovered as muddamal during a patrol. The applicant was arrested on 11 July 2026 and moved the High Court under Section 483 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 after roughly three months in custody. His counsel pressed four points: false implication, substantial completion of the investigation, nothing left to recover, and clean antecedents. The State opposed, citing the gravity of wildlife trafficking, flight risk, and the fact that the chargesheet was yet to be filed.</p>

<h2>The factors that carried the day</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Investigation stage</strong><p>With the seizure already effected and investigation substantially complete, continued custody served no investigative purpose — the recurring fulcrum of post-recovery bail jurisprudence.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Parity</strong><p>A co-accused attributed a similar role had been granted bail. Applying the Gujarat line of authority on parity, equal treatment of equally placed accused tipped the balance.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Antecedents and delay</strong><p>No prior cases, and a trial unlikely to conclude soon. Citing Sanjay Chandra v. CBI and Gudikanti Narasimhulu, the court reiterated that pre-trial detention cannot become a form of punishment.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Bail as rule, structured by conditions</h2>
<p>The order is a textbook illustration of how courts reconcile liberty with the State\'s legitimate concerns: release was granted, but on a lattice of conditions — passport surrender, territorial restriction to Gujarat, staged police-station attendance (weekly until the chargesheet, monthly for six months after), identity and address disclosure, and a bar on obstructing the investigation or tampering with evidence. The court expressly preserved the trial court\'s independence from its preliminary observations, and release remains subject to the applicant not being required in any other offence. Conditions of this kind are not ornamental; breach is a stand-alone ground for cancellation under Section 483(3) BNSS, independent of the merits.</p>

<h2>Why wildlife bail matters beyond Gujarat</h2>
<p>Wildlife-article prosecutions — ambergris, pangolin scales, star tortoises, red sanders — have risen across Indian ports and coastal districts, and Delhi\'s courts see their share through airport interceptions. The framework this order applies travels well: absent a statutory twin-condition bar, the inquiry remains the familiar one of gravity, role, recovery status, antecedents, parity and trial horizon. For the defence, the practical lesson is to crystallise the record on what remains to be investigated; for the prosecution, opposition grounded only in the seriousness of the offence, without case-specific flight or tampering material, increasingly fails once recovery is complete. The constitutional undertone — Article 21\'s guarantee that liberty yields only to necessity shown — is doing more visible work in bail orders across High Courts, and this decision sits squarely in that trend.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>This article describes a judicial order for informational purposes. Bail outcomes are intensely fact-specific: the stage of investigation, the applicant\'s role and antecedents, and the statute invoked can each change the analysis entirely.</p>
</div>
